// Fixture: Model-A wait-orders-them — `wait fa()` forces fa before fb even though they
// are otherwise independent suspending calls with different sleep durations.
//
// `fa` waits 100ms then prints "A". `fb` waits 50ms then prints "B".
// The explicit `wait` before `fa()` forces fa to complete before the next statement
// (fb) begins — even though fa takes longer than fb.
//
// Under BOTH default-parallel and --no-auto-parallel mode the output is "A\nB\ndone"
// because the `wait` is an ordering barrier: it resets the independent group,
// forces fa to completion, then fb runs after.
//
// This proves `wait` is the user's ordering tool (design/concurrency.md lines 97-120):
// writing `wait fa()` guarantees fa completes before fb starts, regardless of durations.
//
// Expected default output:  A\nB\ndone
// Expected sequential output: A\nB\ndone
function fa() -> nothing {
  wait sleep(100)
  print(`A`)
}

function fb() -> nothing {
  wait sleep(50)
  print(`B`)
}

function entrypoint() -> nothing {
  wait fa()
  fb()
  print(`done`)
}
